Answer: **theia**

theia is a research‑grade technique (presented in the acm‑transactions‑on‑software‑engineering‑and‑methodology article, july 2025) that automatically detects **structural bugs** in deep‑learning (dl) programs.  

| aspect | description |
|--------|-------------|
| **goal** | locate bugs that stem from the *design* of a dl model—e.g., wrong or missing activation functions, incorrect layer properties, inappropriate loss functions, faulty data‑pre‑processing, or misplaced/redundant layers. these bugs usually cause low or “stuck” accuracy during training. |
| **how it works** | 1. **meta‑model construction** – builds a library‑agnostic representation of a dl program that captures both **dataset characteristics** (dimensionality, type of training data, etc.) and **model properties** (number of convolutional layers, learning‑rate, etc.). <br>2. **context‑sensitive static analysis** – applies call‑strings analysis and parameter‑sensitive analysis (as described in references [72] and [83]) together with a set of **verification rules** that encode known relationships between dataset traits and model structure. <br>3. **bug localization & repair advice** – when a rule is violated, theia reports the exact location in the source code and suggests how to fix the structural issue. |
| **supported frameworks** | works with the two most popular dl libraries: **keras** and **pytorch**. |
| **when it runs** | at the **beginning of the training process**, before the model has spent significant time on epochs, so developers get early feedback and can avoid wasted computation. |
| **evaluation results** | - tested on **40 real‑world buggy dl programs** collected from stack overflow (both regression and classification
